Turn on the spare 2.8 V rail, to see whether the focus motor lives there

The phone's own factory file says the rear camera carries a focus actuator, a
Dongwoon `ofilm_dw9800`. But scanning the CCI bus with the camera powered and a
capture running, only two chips answer:

    0x1a   the sensor, identifier 0x0682 confirmed
    0x50   the EEPROM

The motor does not respond at any of the typical Dongwoon addresses
-0x0c, 0x0e, 0x0f, 0x18, 0x1c, 0x1e, 0x24, 0x28, 0x36, 0x51, 0x54-. An unpowered
chip does not answer, so the first thing to do is rule that out.

WHICH RAILS THERE ARE, AND WHICH ONE WAS LEFT

The device tree declares four camera regulators that NOBODY uses, all at 2.8 V,
which is the typical voltage for an actuator. And looking at their state on the
phone:

    cam_avdd1        2.8 V   already ON
    cam_avdd2        2.8 V   already ON
    uw_ldo_ois_drv   2.8 V   off          <- the only one missing
    wide_ldo_avdd2   2.8 V   off, but that one is used by the sensor

So the camera PMIC rails are already live and the motor is still silent. The only
candidate left is `uw_ldo_ois_drv`, on TLMM GPIO 65.

It is turned on with `regulator-always-on` because its GPIO is owned by the
kernel and there is no way to drive it from user space.

THIS IS AN EXPERIMENT, NOT THE SOLUTION. If a new chip appears on the bus with
the rail on, that rail will become the declared supply of the actuator node and
this goes away. If nothing appears, it also goes away, and the motor will have to
be looked for on another bus.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
@@ -31,6 +31,22 @@
 		regulator-name = "uw_ldo_ois_drv";
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <2800000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <2800000>;
+		/*
+		 * POWERED ON TO LOOK FOR THE FOCUS MOTOR.
+		 *
+		 * The factory file says the rear camera has an actuator
+		 * -ofilm_dw9800- but scanning the CCI bus with the camera
+		 * powered only the sensor (0x1a) and the EEPROM (0x50) answer.
+		 * An unpowered chip does not answer, and this is the only camera
+		 * 2.8 V rail that was still off: cam_avdd1 and cam_avdd2 already
+		 * come up powered_on from the factory.
+		 *
+		 * It is always enabled because its GPIO is owned by the kernel and there is no
+		 * way to move it from userspace. It is temporary: if
+		 * the motor shows up, it will become the declared supply of the
+		 * actuator node; if it does not, this is removed.
+		 */
+		regulator-always-on;
 
 		gpio = <&tlmm 65 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		startup-delay-us = <100>;
